Defining a subclass registers it automatically and validates it at import time, so a broken check fails before any test runs. A **metaclass** is a subclass of type that customises class creation itself. Example: enforcing that every test class has a docstring:

```code
class DocumentedMeta(type):
    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):
        if bases and not namespace.get("__doc__"):
            raise TypeError(f"class {name} needs a docstring")
        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)
class BaseTest(metaclass=DocumentedMeta):
    pass
class TestBoot(BaseTest):
    """Verify the DUT boots to a login prompt within 300 s."""
try:
    class TestUndocumented(BaseTest):
        pass
except TypeError as e:
    print(e)
