**Factory fixture.** make_sensor returns a function, so one test can create as many objects as it needs, and the fixture still cleans up all of them:

```code
$ pytest -q -s test_adv.py::test_two_sensors
--> start test_two_sensors
.
cleanup: removing 2 simulated sensors
1 passed in 0.01s
```

-s lets the print output through. The --> start line comes from log_test_name, which has autouse=True: it runs for every test in its scope without being requested. Use autouse sparingly (logging, resetting global state), because hidden fixtures make tests harder to understand. **Parametrized fixture.** drive has params=[...], so every test that uses it runs once per parameter, and request.param holds the current value:

```code
$ pytest -q -k "drive or target"
....
[100%]
4 passed, 3 deselected in 0.01s
```

Four tests: test_drive_present three times (NVMe0, NVMe1, NVMe2) plus test_target. **The request object** gives a fixture information about the requesting test: request.param, request.node.name, request.config (command-line options), `request.addfinalizer(func).`

**Built-in fixtures** you will use constantly: tmp_path (a fresh pathlib.Path folder per test), tmp_path_factory (the session-scoped version), monkeypatch, capsys (captured stdout/stderr), caplog (captured logging records), request, pytestconfig. List all fixtures available to a test with pytest --fixtures.

