Walk-through:

- agent none at the top and an agent per stage lets each stage run on the right machine: unit tests on any Linux agent, simulator tests in throwaway Docker containers, hardware tests on the agent wired to rack 12.

- parameters appear as a form in “Build with Parameters”. Inside steps they are available as params.SUITE in Groovy and as environment variables ($SUITE) in sh. Quote them in shell ("$FIRMWARE") because parameter values are user input (Chapter 28 on injection).

- cron('H 2 * * 1-5') schedules a nightly run between 02:00 and 02:59 on weekdays; H hashes the job name into a fixed minute so that 50 nightly jobs do not all start at 02:00.

- when skips the hardware stage unless the user ticked RUN_HARDWARE or the build was started by the timer. (triggeredBy is a standard when condition in declarative pipelines.)

- lock(resource: 'rack12-u07') waits until no other build holds that device. Two jobs flashing the same BMC at once is a common and expensive lab accident.

- The hardware post { always { ... } } collects BMC logs and powers the DUT off **even when tests fail or the build is aborted**, with || true so a failing clean-up step does not hide the real result. Leaving a rack in an unknown state breaks the next team’s run.

- archiveArtifacts keeps logs, reports and captures with the build; fingerprint: true records checksums so files can be traced across jobs. buildDiscarder prevents old builds from filling the controller’s disk.

- cleanWs() in cleanup deletes the workspace after everything else, keeping agents clean.

