Validating input prevents confusing failures and injection problems when values end up in commands.

getopts (POSIX built-in) parses short options like -h -t 5 -o out.json.

```code
timeout_s=5; output=""; verbose=0
while getopts ":ht:o:v" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    h) usage; exit 0 ;;
    t) timeout_s="$OPTARG" ;;
    o) output="$OPTARG" ;;
    v) verbose=1 ;;
    :) echo "Option -$OPTARG needs a value" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    \?) echo "Unknown option -$OPTARG" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND - 1))     # remaining non-option arguments now in "$@"
```

The leading : in ":ht:o:v" enables silent error handling so your :/ \? branches print the messages. A colon after a letter means “takes a value”. getopts doesn’t support long options ( --timeout); write a manual while case loop for those.

