A version control system (VCS) records every change to a set of files: **what** changed, **who** changed it, **when**, and **why** (the commit message). With it you can:

- go back to the exact test code that ran in a regression three weeks ago;

- see which change made a test start failing;

- let five engineers edit the same suite without overwriting each other;

- give Jenkins a precise, repeatable input: “run commit 7341583”.

Without version control, test teams end up with folders like tests_final, tests_final_v2 and tests_final_v2_ravi_fix. Nobody can say which one ran in last night’s regression. In embedded validation this is worse, because a result is only meaningful if you know **three** versions: the firmware version, the test-code version, and the configuration version. Git gives you the last two precisely.

On the hosting service, protect main and release/* so that:

- direct pushes are blocked (changes go through pull requests);

- at least one approving review is required;

- the CI check (your Jenkins pipeline) must pass;

- force pushes and deletion are blocked.

Chapter 4 shows the GitHub settings; GitLab (“protected branches”) and Gerrit (access rights + Verified label) have equivalents.

A **quality gate** is a rule the pipeline enforces before allowing the next step:

- all smoke tests pass before hardware regression starts;

- no new test failures compared with the baseline;

- coverage of the framework code ≥ 80 %;

- JUnit report exists and is parseable (a missing report is a failure, not a pass);

- firmware version on the device equals the version under test.

Gates must be **automatic** and **explainable**: the console says which gate failed and why.

**Declarative** is a structured, opinionated syntax inside pipeline { }. Jenkins validates its structure before running, and it reads like a specification. Use it by default.

**Scripted** is plain Groovy around node { } blocks. It is fully flexible and less validated. Use it for genuinely dynamic logic (loops creating stages, complex retries), usually inside a script { } block or a shared library function.

```code
// Declarative                                   // Scripted
pipeline {                                       node('python3') {
  agent { label 'python3' }                        stage('Test') {
  stages {                                           checkout scm
    stage('Test') {                                  sh 'python3 -m pytest'
      steps { sh 'python3 -m pytest' }             }
    }                                            }
  }
}
```

Both run on the same engine. Pipeline code is transformed so that it can pause and resume (“CPS”): this is why some normal Groovy features (e.g. non-serializable objects like regex Matcher kept in variables) cause errors. Keep logic in pipelines simple; put real logic in scripts and Python.

